`@import("zml/user-guide")` exports `Tensor`, `Shape`, `Buffer`, `Platform`, and `Exe`.
These API pages follow the revision used in the [ZML quickstart]($link.page('zml/user-guide/quickstart')).

# [Declare a symbolic tensor]($heading.id('tensor'))

Use `zml.Tensor` for a symbolic input, weight, or intermediate result.
[Tensor source](https://github.com/zml/zml/blob/8fcff8c462070d06056f8a85ba0c8a81f0f3a636/zml/tensor.zig)

- `zml.Tensor.init(dimensions, dtype)`\
  `Tensor`. Declare a symbolic tensor from dimensions and a `zml.DataType` such as `.f32`.
- `zml.Tensor.fromShape(shape)`\
  `Tensor`. Declare a symbolic tensor from an existing [Shape]($link.page('zml/api-reference/shape')).

# [Inspect dimensions and tags]($heading.id('dimensions-and-tags'))

- `tensor.shape()`\
  `Shape`. Read the tensor's shape metadata.
- `tensor.withTags(tags)`\
  `Tensor`. Return the tensor with one tag per axis, in axis order.
- `tensor.axis(axis)` / `tensor.dim(axis)`\
  `u3` index / `i64` size. Select an axis by index or tag.

# [Contract named axes]($heading.id('contract-named-axes'))

Use operations such as `dot` inside the function ZML traces during compilation.

- `lhs.dot(rhs, tags)`\
  `Tensor`. Contract the named axes in both operands. Their sizes and the operands' element types must match. Other shared tags become batch axes.

See the [named-axis example]($link.page('zml/developer-guide/tensors-and-shapes').ref('tagged-dot-product'))
for how contraction determines the result shape.
